4.8 Check duplicate rows
The file should contain one row per reported holding. Count combinations of company, country, and industry to find possible repetition:

gpfg |&gt;
  count(company, country, industry) |&gt;
  filter(n &gt; 1)






count() creates n, the number of matching rows. Inspect the result in the original table:

gpfg |&gt;
  filter(company == "3P Learning Ltd")






The two complete rows are identical. After confirming that, use distinct() without column names to keep each complete row once:

gpfg &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  distinct()

A repeated company name is not always a duplicate. The decision depends on the meaning and values of the complete row.

turn a broad topic into a precise reporting question;
inspect a clean table before analysing it;
distinguish description, ranking, comparison, relationship, and trend questions;
calculate summaries with summarise();
rank rows with arrange();
compare groups with group_by() and summarise(); and
turn a calculated result into a carefully worded finding.

5.7 Ask a ranking question

Which ten individual holdings had the largest reported market values in 2025?

The question needs the company, market, industry, and NOK value. select() keeps those columns. arrange() orders the rows, and desc() requests descending order:

largest_holdings &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  select(company, country, industry, market_value_nok) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_nok)) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 10)

largest_holdings






slice_head(n = 10) keeps the first ten rows after sorting. A ranking identifies large observations; it does not explain why they are large. Verify important rows against the source before publication.

5.8 Ask a comparison question

Which investment markets accounted for the largest total reported market values in 2025?

This question needs one total per country. group_by(country) forms the country groups, and summarise() returns one result row for each group:

country_summary &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  group_by(country) |&gt;
  summarise(
    holding_records = n(),
    market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok)
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_nok))

country_summary |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 10)






Track the changing row meaning:

before summarise(), one row represents one holding;
after summarise(), one row represents one investment market.

Inside summarise(), n() counts holding records and sum() adds their values. These measures need not produce the same ranking.

Calculate a share
mutate() can add each market’s percentage share of the full reported value:

country_summary &lt;- country_summary |&gt;
  mutate(
    share_percent = market_value_nok / sum(market_value_nok) * 100
  )

country_summary |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 10)






The denominator is the sum of the country totals. The resulting percentage answers: “What share of the 2025 reported total is associated with this investment market?”

explain why trend questions require multiple time points;
import several annual CSV files;
compare their names and types before combining them;
combine tables with the same columns using bind_rows();
inspect time coverage, record counts, categories, and missing values;
calculate annual and country-year summaries; and
interpret changes without confusing holdings with flows or returns.

6.8 Ask an overall trend question

How did the total reported market value of the equity holdings change from 2021 through 2025?

The question needs year and market_value_nok. Group the observations by year and return one summary row for each annual snapshot:

annual_summary &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  group_by(year) |&gt;
  summarise(
    holding_records = n(),
    market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok)
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(year)

annual_summary






Track the changing row meaning:

before summarise(), one row represents one holding in one year;
after summarise(), one row represents one annual snapshot.

Keeping both record count and market value visible helps us notice that a change in the total occurs alongside a changing number of reported holdings.

6.9 Combine trend and comparison
We can also ask:

How did reported market value change for the United States, China, and Japan from 2021 through 2025?

Create a vector containing the three selected markets, then use %in% to keep rows whose country appears in that vector:

selected_countries &lt;- c("United States", "China", "Japan")

country_trends &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  filter(country %in% selected_countries) |&gt;
  group_by(year, country) |&gt;
  summarise(market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok)) |&gt;
  arrange(country, year)

country_trends






One row now represents one selected investment market in one year. %in% asks whether each country belongs to selected_countries.
This table combines a comparison question—differences among markets—with a trend question—change across years. Chapter 8 will display the three series with separate colored lines.

6.10 Interpret trends carefully
The annual values are year-end market values of reported holdings. A change can reflect:

security prices;
exchange rates;
purchases or sales;
companies entering or leaving the portfolio; or
changes in reporting coverage or classification.

The table does not isolate those causes. Describe the observed pattern first, then seek additional evidence before explaining it. Use “year-end market value,” not “money invested that year” or “investment return.”






The complete history is optional



data/gpfg_history.csv.gz contains the full 1998–2025 archive. It is useful for advanced reporting, but the longer period includes major coverage and classification changes. The five-year table is the main beginner dataset.

